Policy "No eval":
Never call JavaScript's eval() or new Function() on strings. Other APIs that happen to be named eval, such as Redis scripting, are not covered.

<untrusted_code>
File: src/orders.ts
Lines: 19-19
Rule message: Possible violation of the policy "No eval".

Code (flagged lines marked >>):
       1 | import { Request, Response } from "express";
       2 | import { db } from "./db";
       3 | import { redis } from "./cache";
       4 | 
       5 | const ORDERS_TABLE = "orders";
       6 | 
       7 | export async function getOrder(req: Request, res: Response) {
       8 |   const rows = await db.query(`SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = '${req.params.id}'`);
       9 |   res.json(rows[0]);
      10 | }
      11 | 
      12 | export async function countOrders(): Promise<number> {
      13 |   const rows = await db.query(`SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM ${ORDERS_TABLE}`);
      14 |   return rows[0].n;
      15 | }
      16 | 
      17 | export async function applyDiscount(req: Request, res: Response) {
      18 |   const formula = req.body.formula;
>>    19 |   const price = eval(formula);
      20 |   res.json({ price });
      21 | }
      22 | 
      23 | const RELEASE_LOCK = `if redis.call("get", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then return redis.call("del", KEYS[1]) end return 0`;
      24 | 
      25 | export async function releaseLock(key: string, owner: string) {
      26 |   return redis.eval(RELEASE_LOCK, 1, key, owner);
      27 | }
</untrusted_code>

Why the reviewer kept it: Line 19 calls the global eval() on `formula`, which is taken directly from `req.body.formula`, a user-controlled request field, so it is arbitrary code execution. The redis.eval on line 26 is a Redis scripting API and is not covered by the policy, but line 19 is a genuine violation.

Answer with the JSON object only.
